{"id":"113f847d-5dde-4c88-ad2e-7397a8878d39","object":"chat.completion","created":1784994417,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    calls_sorted = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: c['end'])\\n    n = len(calls_sorted)\\n    ends = [c['end'] for c in calls_sorted]\\n    p = [-1] * n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        start_i = calls_sorted[i]['start']\\n        lo, hi = 0, i - 1\\n        while lo <= hi:\\n            mid = (lo + hi) // 2\\n            if ends[mid] <= start_i:\\n                lo = mid + 1\\n            else:\\n                hi = mid - 1\\n        p[i] = hi\\n    dp = [0] * n\\n    choice = [False] * n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        score = calls_sorted[i]['score']\\n        include = score + (dp[p[i]] if p[i] >= 0 else 0)\\n        exclude = dp[i - 1] if i > 0 else 0\\n        if include >= exclude:\\n            dp[i] = include\\n            choice[i] = True\\n        else:\\n            dp[i] = exclude\\n            choice[i] = False\\n    selected = []\\n    i = n - 1\\n    while i >= 0:\\n        if choice[i]:\\n            selected.append(calls_sorted[i])\\n            i = p[i]\\n        else:\\n            i -= 1\\n    selected.reverse()\\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\\n    new_tokens = []\\n    orig = tokens\\n    idx = 0\\n    for c in selected:\\n        while idx < c['start']:\\n            new_tokens.append(orig[idx])\\n            idx += 1\\n        new_tokens.extend(c['replacement_tokens'])\\n        idx = c['end']\\n    while idx < len(orig):\\n        new_tokens.append(orig[idx])\\n        idx += 1\\n    return {'selected_call_ids': selected_call_ids, 'tokens': new_tokens}\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to understand the task. The user provided an EXPLICIT CASE INTERFACE and EXACT OUTPUT CONTRACT. The task is AGENT-TF-02. We need to produce a JSON with \"implementation\" key containing Python source code defining solve(case). The function takes a case payload (the inner object) and must return a JSON object with selected_call_ids (list of strings) and tokens (list of strings). The output schema says no additional properties.\n\nWe need to infer the logic from the fixtures? The fixture payloads are provided with tokens and calls. Each call has call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. There are three identical payloads but with different nonce. Likely the task is to select non-overlapping calls with highest total score, and then replace the tokens accordingly? Or maybe something else? The output requires selected_call_ids and tokens. In the example, tokens are [\"t0\",\"t1\",...,\"t9\"]. Calls: a (1-3), b (2-5), c (7-9). Overlap between a and b (they overlap at indices 2). Typically in such tasks, we need to select a set of non-overlapping calls to maximize total score, then apply replacements to produce a new tokens list. The output should be the selected call IDs and the resulting tokens after applying replacements in order. But we need to confirm from the schema: selected_call_ids is array of strings, tokens is array of strings. The fixture doesn't show expected output. Only input. So we must deduce the algorithm.\n\nGiven it's a registered procedure task named AGENT-TF-02, likely it's a standard optimization problem: given calls with intervals (start inclusive, end exclusive?), scores, and replacement tokens, choose a set of non-overlapping calls that maximizes total score, then produce the token sequence where the ranges are replaced by the corresponding replacement_tokens. The tokens list in output is the final sequence after all selected replacements applied in order of start (or call order? probably sorted by start). Overlapping calls cannot both be selected because they conflict in token positions.\n\nIn the example: calls: a [1,3) score 0.8, replace t1,t2 with \"<a>\". b [2,5) score 0.9, replace t2,t3,t4 with \"<b>\". c [7,9) score 0.7, replace t7,t8 with \"<c>\". Non-overlapping sets: {a,c} score 1.5; {b} score 0.9; {c} score 0.7; {a} 0.8. So best is {a,c}. Resulting tokens: t0, then replace a: <a>, then what about t3? a ends at 3, so tokens after a: t3,t4,t5,t6, then c replaces t7,t8: <c>, then t9. So final tokens: [\"t0\",\"<a>\",\"t3\",\"t4\",\"t5\",\"t6\",\"<c>\",\"t9\"]? But wait: a replaces positions 1 and 2 (indices 1 and 2) with one token \"<a>\". So after replacement, token at index0 remains, then \"<a>\", then positions originally 3 onward shift? The replacement reduces length: originally from index1 to index2 (2 tokens) become 1 token. Similarly c replaces indices7 and8 (2 tokens) with 1 token. So final length: original 10 tokens, minus 2 from a, minus 2 from c, plus 2 replacement tokens = 8 tokens. My guess: output tokens list would be: t0, <a>, t3, t4, t5, t6, <c>, t9. That is 8 tokens. So selected_call_ids: [\"a\",\"c\"].\n\nBut we need to ensure the algorithm is correct. Since it's the only fixture, that's likely the expected behavior. Also note that calls have start and end indices; likely end is exclusive (end index not included). The replacement_tokens is a list of tokens to insert for that span. The span length may be different from replacement length. So the output tokens should be the result of applying all selected replacements in order of start index (or maybe by call_id? but start order is natural). Also need to handle overlapping intervals: we need to choose a set of non-overlapping calls that maximizes sum of scores. Since multiple calls may have same start/end, but in this example they are distinct.\n\nThus solve(case) should:\n1. Parse case: case has keys \"tokens\" and \"calls\" (and possibly \"_case_nonce\", but ignore).\n2. For each call in calls, have id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens.\n3. Solve maximum weight independent set on intervals (weighted interval scheduling). Overlap condition: intervals [start, end) overlap if they share any index. Must select non-overlapping.\n4. After selecting optimal set (max sum score), sort selected calls by start.\n5. Build the new tokens list: iterate through original tokens, for each call in sorted selected, replace the range with its replacement_tokens. But careful: as we replace, the indices shift. Instead, we can process sequentially: start with original list, for each call (sorted by start), we compute the position in current list corresponding to start index (since earlier replacements might have changed length). But because calls are non-overlapping, and we process in order, we can maintain an offset. Alternatively, we can build from scratch: iterate through original token indices, when we encounter a start of a selected call, we append its replacement_tokens, then skip the original tokens until end. Since calls are non-overlapping and sorted, this works by scanning original indices.\n\nBut note: replacement_tokens may be multiple tokens. So we need to insert them.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- sorted_calls = sorted(selected_calls, key=lambda c: c['start'])\n- new_tokens = []\n- i = 0\n- for call in sorted_calls:\n    while i < call['start']:\n        new_tokens.append(original_tokens[i])\n        i += 1\n    # now i == call['start']\n    # add replacement_tokens\n    new_tokens.extend(call['replacement_tokens'])\n    # skip original tokens until call['end']\n    i = call['end']  # because end is exclusive\n- while i < len(original_tokens):\n    new_tokens.append(original_tokens[i])\n    i += 1\n\nThat yields new tokens.\n\nNow, we need to select optimal set. This is a classic interval scheduling DP. Since number of calls is likely small (maybe up to few hundred), we can implement DP. Sort calls by end time. For each call, compute p(j) = last call that ends before its start (non-overlapping). Then dp[j] = max(score_j + dp[p(j)], dp[j-1]).\n\nWe need to also reconstruct the selected ids. Use dp and keep track.\n\nEdge cases: if multiple calls have same start/end? Overlap defined as intervals that intersect at any point. If start == other's start, they overlap. If end == other's start, they don't overlap because end exclusive? Usually non-overlapping if end <= other.start (since end exclusive). So condition: call_i.end <= call_j.start (or vice versa) for non-overlap.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) returning dict with selected_call_ids and tokens.\n\nImportant: The output must match exactly the schema: selected_call_ids list of strings, tokens list of strings. No extra keys.\n\nWe'll implement without external dependencies, using standard library.\n\nNow, produce the JSON with \"implementation\" key. The Python source must be a string, properly escaped.\n\nWe'll write code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case['tokens']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # sort calls by end for DP\n    calls_sorted = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: c['end'])\n    n = len(calls_sorted)\n    # precompute p: index of last call that ends before start of current call\n    p = [-1] * n\n    ends = [c['end'] for c in calls_sorted]\n    for i in range(n):\n        start_i = calls_sorted[i]['start']\n        # binary search: find largest end < start_i\n        lo, hi = 0, i-1\n        while lo <= hi:\n            mid = (lo+hi)//2\n            if ends[mid] <= start_i:\n                lo = mid+1\n            else:\n                hi = mid-1\n        p[i] = hi  # hi is last index with end <= start_i, or -1 if none\n    dp = [0] * n\n    choice = [False] * n  # whether we include this call in optimal\n    for i in range(n):\n        score = calls_sorted[i]['score']\n        include = score + (dp[p[i]] if p[i]>=0 else 0)\n        exclude = dp[i-1] if i>0 else 0\n        if include >= exclude:\n            dp[i] = include\n            choice[i] = True\n        else:\n            dp[i] = exclude\n            choice[i] = False\n    # reconstruction\n    selected = []\n    i = n-1\n    while i >= 0:\n        if choice[i]:\n            selected.append(calls_sorted[i])\n            i = p[i]  # go to previous compatible\n        else:\n            i -= 1\n    selected.reverse()  # sorted by start (since sorted by end, but we processed in order, reversed)\n    # Actually, selected is built from last to first, so reverse to get increasing start.\n    selected_call_ids = [c['call_id'] for c in selected]\n    # Build new tokens\n    new_tokens = []\n    orig = tokens\n    idx = 0\n    for c in selected:\n        while idx < c['start']:\n            new_tokens.append(orig[idx])\n            idx += 1\n        new_tokens.extend(c['replacement_tokens'])\n        idx = c['end']  # skip original tokens of the span\n    while idx < len(orig):\n        new_tokens.append(orig[idx])\n        idx += 1\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": new_tokens}\n\nCheck for edge: scores can be floats, but we compare using >=, fine. No need for deterministic tie-breaking; if equal, we include this call (since we used >=). That's okay.\n\nNow, ensure we don't mutate case. We only read.\n\nMake sure to handle case when no calls are selected (should return empty list and original tokens). The DP handles that (dp will be 0, choice false for all, selected empty, new_tokens becomes original tokens). Good.\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON with \"implementation\" key containing that Python source as a string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll construct the JSON manually or using json.dumps.\n\nI'll write the Python source as a multiline string and then json.dumps it.\n\nBut careful: The response must be entire response exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we will produce that JSON.\n\nLet's write the answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1374,"completion_tokens":2905,"total_tokens":4279,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2406},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1280,"prompt_cache_miss